1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,166
Let's write one line of C: a = b + c;

2
00:00:10,166 --> 00:00:15,766
But a CPU doesn't understand C. All it can run is strings of 0s and 1s.

3
00:00:15,766 --> 00:00:22,100
Two translation steps sit in between. First the compiler turns C into assembly language...

4
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:27,900
...then the assembler turns each assembly instruction into a 32-bit machine instruction.

5
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:35,533
Assembly maps almost one-to-one onto machine code: it's machine instructions written out as text.

6
00:00:35,533 --> 00:00:43,933
The specification of which instructions a CPU supports and which registers it has is called its instruction set architecture (ISA).

7
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:53,800
It's a contract between software and hardware: software uses only these instructions, and hardware guarantees to execute them correctly.

8
00:00:53,800 --> 00:01:03,533
x86, ARM and RISC-V are all ISAs. CS61C uses RISC-V: open-source, simple and regular.

9
00:01:03,533 --> 00:01:10,766
RISC means a reduced instruction set: few, regular instructions, so the hardware can be simple and fast.

10
00:01:10,766 --> 00:01:18,666
The RV32I base instruction set has only about 40 instructions, and this series covers most of them.

11
00:01:18,666 --> 00:01:25,666
RISC-V arithmetic works only on registers: a tiny amount of very fast storage inside the CPU.

12
00:01:25,666 --> 00:01:31,766
RV32I has 32 integer registers: x0 through x31.

13
00:01:31,766 --> 00:01:40,133
Each register is 32 bits wide. 32 bits, or 4 bytes, is called a word.

14
00:01:40,133 --> 00:01:43,633
Why only 32? Because smaller is faster.

15
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:53,600
A register can be read or written within one clock cycle; a memory access is often 100 times slower or more.

16
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:58,833
One of them, x0, is special: its value is always 0.

17
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:02,633
Anything written to x0 is silently thrown away.

18
00:02:02,633 --> 00:02:07,133
Sounds wasteful? You'll soon see how handy it is.

19
00:02:07,133 --> 00:02:12,266
In assembly we usually call registers by their other names, the ABI names.

20
00:02:12,266 --> 00:02:15,333
Names starting with t are temporary registers...

21
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:18,133
those starting with s are saved registers...

22
00:02:18,133 --> 00:02:21,766
and the a registers pass arguments and return values.

23
00:02:21,766 --> 00:02:30,933
ra, sp and a few others have special jobs. We'll cover the rules for all these registers with function calls in Episode 7.

24
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:38,366
Arithmetic instructions have a rigid format: operation name, destination register, then two source registers.

25
00:02:38,366 --> 00:02:45,933
add rd, rs1, rs2 simply means rd = rs1 + rs2.

26
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:50,233
For example, say s1 holds 7 and s2 holds 5.

27
00:02:50,233 --> 00:02:55,733
Run add s0, s1, s2: both values go into the adder...

28
00:02:55,733 --> 00:02:59,633
...and the result, 12, is written to s0.

29
00:02:59,633 --> 00:03:09,300
sub works the same way: rd = rs1 − rs2. Order matters: rs2 is subtracted from rs1.

30
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:14,433
Now let's compile something a bit bigger: a = b + c - d.

31
00:03:14,433 --> 00:03:22,500
Suppose the compiler has put a, b, c and d in s0, s1, s2 and s3.

32
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,833
Each instruction does only one operation, so this takes two steps, with the intermediate result in the temporary register t0.

33
00:03:30,833 --> 00:03:39,533
Let b = 10, c = 20, d = 5. Step one: t0 = 10 + 20 = 30.

34
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:48,100
Step two: s0 = t0 − d = 30 − 5 = 25. Done!

35
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:52,200
What if we're adding a constant, as in a = b + 5?

36
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:58,200
Use addi. The i stands for immediate: the constant is encoded right in the instruction.

37
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:03,466
RISC-V has no subi: subtracting a constant is just adding a negative one.

38
00:04:03,466 --> 00:04:14,300
addi's immediate is only 12 bits, so it ranges from −2048 to 2047. Why 12 bits? Episode 9 explains.

39
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:21,166
Now for x0's trick. The assembler offers pseudoinstructions, which it replaces with real instructions.

40
00:04:21,166 --> 00:04:25,766
mv adds 0; li starts from x0 and adds a constant...

41
00:04:25,766 --> 00:04:29,933
...and nop writes its result to x0, so nothing changes.

42
00:04:29,933 --> 00:04:36,266
One register that's always 0 saves several dedicated instructions. That's RISC thinking.

